  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nA generally quiet weather period is expected through the \nremainder of the week. Near to slightly below normal highs \nmainly in the 60s and 70s will gradually warm back into the 70s \nand 80s as we head into the weekend. Mainly dry conditions are \nanticipated to prevail, though chances for showers and \npotentially some thunderstorms will develop early Wednesday into\nThursday, and again late Saturday into Sunday.\n\nFor today, an upper level low cutting across South Dakota is \ninducing scattered showers across portions of western North \nDakota. As high pressure builds in across the International \nBorder late this afternoon into this evening, this low will \nbegin to dive to the southeast, limiting precpitation chances to\nmainly the southwest through the early overnight period. \nOverall, only light amounts of accumulation are expected through\nthe remainder of today, with the probability to exceed another \n0.25\" of precip across the far southwest only around 30% or so \n(as per the latest calibrated NBM 5.0). Otherwise for today, \nhighs from the mid 50s west to mid 60s central are forecast for \nthis afternoon. With overcast skies expected to gradually clear \nto the southeast along with the rain through the late afternoon \ninto the overnight period, low temperatures tonight will \nbe mainly into the 40s, with portions of the Turtle Mountain \npotentially dropping as low as the upper 30s.\n\nConditions through the remainder of the week are expected to be\ncharacterized by prevailing southwesterly flow over the \nnorthern Plains. While mainly dry conditions are anticipated \nover the forecast area with this flow pattern, the ensemble does\nattempt to resolve a modest shortwave trough cutting across the\nregion early Wednesday into Thursday, which can be expected to \npromote at least low to medium chances (33 to 55 percent) for \nshowers and potentially some isolated thunder at times through \nthat period. The potential for severe weather remains very low. \nThe greatest source of disagreement within the ensemble remains \nhow long precpitation will persist over the forecast area \nthrough Thursday. A slight majority (approximately 60 percent of\nensemble members) favor showers persisting well into Thursday \nafternoon over central North Dakota as the upper level flow \npattern is slow to eject the shortwave out into the southern \nCanadian Prairies, compared to a slight minority of members \n(approximately 40 percent) which instead favor the rapidly \ndevelopment of an upper level ridge over the southern Great \nPlains that helps kick the inciting trough well out of the \nforecast area by late Wednesday evening. In either scenario, \nonly light precpitation amounts are expected with the stratiform\nshowers that do develop. However, considering moderately high \nmodel PWATs around 1.25\" Wednesday afternoon, some locally \nhigher rain totals can be anticipated when and where \nthunderstorms do end up developing. \n\nBy early Thursday through Friday, transient upper level ridging\nis expected to slide over the northern Plains, promoting \nseasonably warm high temperatures mainly in the mid 70s to mid \n80s. Conditions through Saturday will thus remain fairly dry, \nbefore the ridge is progged to break down and flow over the \nnorthern Plains becomes cyclonic as a fairly robust upper level \nlow moves across the Canadian Prairies starting Saturday. While \nthe ensemble is fairly cohesive on the timing of this system, \nthere is a split between a more northerly (thus drier) track \nversus a more southerly (thus wetter) track as of this forecast \ncycle. As it stands, at least low to medium chances for showers \nand some storms can be anticipated Saturday through Sunday. With\nnear zonal to slight northwesterly flow then expected to \ndevelop heading into next week, a return to seasonable highs can\nalso be anticipated with and behind this weekend system.",
